 of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-3x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=3x+2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+2x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=3-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c17c81b4abc0d4ebd1eadfe951b0eb0c2674d9eb07b785e7fd972fd00e513ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-3x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=3x+2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+2x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=3-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c17c81b4abc0d4ebd1eadfe951b0eb0c2674d9eb07b785e7fd972fd00e513ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371906 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ u=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=4 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ u=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=4 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371913 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371974 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2d98eb2de472226d010fb633478f3662754679c2256e3fd3d06600a6599bc7f5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2d98eb2de472226d010fb633478f3662754679c2256e3fd3d06600a6599bc7f5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372000 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [:db/cas 17592186372000 :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-5|=5.\n$$" "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-5|=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372007 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [:db/cas 17592186372007 :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$" "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372293 :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372300 :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372307 :question/problem "![](images/db/db042c1d6bc23ed95988fe1a83a565065928dca06ffe454225da6c638a70f741.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "![](images/db/db042c1d6bc23ed95988fe1a83a565065928dca06ffe454225da6c638a70f741.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372543 :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ."] [:db/cas 17592186372698 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186372698 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\, \\\\ \\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\, \\\\ \\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372703 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186372703 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372705 :answer/value "[\\begin{al