 \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379444 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379478 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379478 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+t-1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (t+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot (t+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}t & t+1 \\\\ 1-t & -t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot (-t)-(t+1)\\cdot (1-t) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}-1+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (1-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+t-1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot (t+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot (t+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t+1 \\\\ 1-t & -t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot (-t)-(t+1)\\cdot (1-t) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}-1+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."] [:db/cas 17592186379803 :question/problem "Find ${M}^{32},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "Find ${M}^{32},$ where\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379803 :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{32}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\