$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -10 \\\\ -10 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186381076 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=5+20+1=26.\n$$" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 20\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=5+20+1=26.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186381083 :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186381083 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}32 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}304 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}72 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}720 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}72 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}720 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 72\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}720 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}1\\,440 \\\\ 720\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{4}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}304 \\\\ 33 \\\\ 23\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}72 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}720 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}72 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}720 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}720 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\,440 \\\\ 720\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186381085 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186381086 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186381087 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/ca