he $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}-{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})+5(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}-5{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-8{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186382416 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382417 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382418 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382419 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382420 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382427 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=2{b}_{2}?$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=2{b}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186382427 :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+7{b}_{3} \\\\  & =7{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-7(-{b}_{3})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1})-(2{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+7{b}_{3} \\\\  & =7{b}_{3}+4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-7(-{b}_{3})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1})-(2{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186382429 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382430 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382431 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382432 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382433 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382434 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186382434 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}+4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})+4(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{16}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}+4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})+4(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{16}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186382436 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382437 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382438 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382439 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382440 :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382518 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382518 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\text{ }-2\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}21 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,-10 \\\\ -10 & \\text{ }21\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\text{ }-2\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & \\,-10 \\\\ -10 & \\text{ }21\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & \\text{ }1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ -4 & \\,\\,-2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,1 & \\text{ }0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382520 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382521 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382522 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382523 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382524 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382552 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a-b.$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a-b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186382552 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=1-1=0.$" "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=1-1=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186382559 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382561 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382562 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382563 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382564 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382565 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186382566 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382566 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\sqrt{26}.\n$$" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\sqrt{26}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382573 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$